{
  "commit_id": "0c0ad56aec1aa02d8fda3fcb4fdc1621b4904ec2",
  "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR adds a Windows-native `install.ps1` with solid PS 5.1/7 dual-runtime care: child-scoped preference changes for `irm | iex`, TLS 1.2 for GitHub, SHA-256 verification against `checksums.txt`, both `entire.exe` and `git-remote-entire.exe`, and Scoop delegation for stable. Direct-install PATH handling is the main correctness gap — conflict detection runs before the promised PATH prepend and still does not enumerate every `entire` on PATH, so the documented nightly-with-Scoop path fails after the files are copied. CI only runs `-Help`, which is a useful parse check on both engines but would not catch any of those runtime bugs.\n\n## Issue counts by severity\n\n- bugs: 2\n- suggestions: 3\n- nits: 1",
  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "scripts/install.ps1",
      "line": 376,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[bug]** PATH conflict detection runs before `Add-ToUserPath`, so a direct install throws whenever another `entire` is already first on PATH — and never prepends the new directory. That is the advertised nightly path when Scoop is present: the script warns that Scoop has no nightly, copies the verified nightly into `%USERPROFILE%\\.local\\bin`, then errors with \"PATH needs adjustment\" because Scoop's shim still wins. The README promises this flow \"add[s] that directory to your user PATH if needed\"; prepending would have made the new binary win on User PATH. The files are left on disk, Scoop's stable still runs, and the auto PATH update is dead for the case that most needs it.\n\n**Suggestion:** Call `Add-ToUserPath` first (unless `-NoPathUpdate`), then re-run `Get-Command`. Warn if other copies remain; throw only if the first-on-PATH binary is still not the one just installed (e.g. a Machine PATH entry that User PATH cannot beat)."
    },
    {
      "path": "scripts/install.ps1",
      "line": 351,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[bug]** `Get-Command \"entire\" -CommandType Application` without `-All` returns only the first Application on PATH. The follow-up commit that \"fix[ed] PATH conflict detection to find all entire binaries\" therefore still cannot see a Scoop shim (or any other copy) once `~\\.local\\bin` is already first. The \"takes priority, but consider removing the other installation\" branch is unreachable, which is exactly the prior-direct-install + Scoop-shim scenario that commit described.\n\n**Suggestion:** Use `Get-Command \"entire\" -CommandType Application -All -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue` (and keep the `@()` wrap). Then `$conflicting` actually lists every other `entire.exe`."
    },
    {
      "path": ".github/workflows/ci.yml",
      "line": 86,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[suggestion]** The new Windows steps only run `.\\scripts\\install.ps1 -Help` under `powershell` and `pwsh`. That catches parse errors and confirms both engines, but it never exercises the documented `irm | iex` / `[scriptblock]::Create` invocation, Scoop delegation, checksum verification, zip extraction, binary copy, `entire version`, or PATH update. Issues 1 and 2 would ship green.\n\n**Suggestion:** At least invoke via `& ([scriptblock]::Create((Get-Content -Raw .\\scripts\\install.ps1))) -Help` so the one-liner path is parsed the same way users run it. Stronger: a real install to `$env:RUNNER_TEMP\\entire-bin` with `-Channel stable -NoPathUpdate` (and assert `entire.exe` / `git-remote-entire.exe` plus `entire version`), which GitHub-hosted runners can do against public releases."
    },
    {
      "path": "README.md",
      "line": 70,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[suggestion]** `install.ps1` is now the documented Windows default, but the in-CLI updater still treats a non-Scoop Windows binary as having no native installer (`versioncheck.canAutoInstall` / `MaybeAutoUpdate` print the GitHub releases page, and the comment there still says there is no native equivalent). A user who installed via this script and later sees \"Update available\" will not be pointed at the command in this README.\n\n**Suggestion:** Teach `updateCommand` / `canAutoInstall` about the PowerShell installer (direct-install path → the `irm | iex` / `-Channel nightly` one-liners; leave Scoop as-is). Until then, the README should not be the only place that command lives."
    },
    {
      "path": "scripts/install.ps1",
      "line": 81,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[suggestion]** Stable+Scoop uses `scoop prefix entire` to decide install vs update. Users still on the pre-rename `cli` package will look uninstalled, so the script runs `scoop install entire/entire` without `uninstall entire/cli` / `scoop reset entire`. That is the shim-fight the README's migration section exists to avoid.\n\n**Suggestion:** If `scoop prefix entire` fails, try `scoop prefix cli` and print the documented migration command instead of a plain install."
    },
    {
      "path": "scripts/install.ps1",
      "line": 403,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "**[nit]** The catch block prints `Error: ...` and then `throw`s. For `irm | iex` in an interactive session that surfaces as the same message twice (once in red, once as the terminating error).\n\n**Suggestion:** After printing, `exit 1` when running as a file, or `throw` without the Write-Host (or rethrow as a `SilentError`-style object and not print twice)."
    }
  ]
}

