No. After the follow-up it is a no-op.

It existed to keep Windows unknown installs off `curl | bash` (that cannot run from cmd/PowerShell) and send them to the GitHub releases page instead. Once unknown Windows has `install.ps1`, every path `updateCommand` can produce is printable, so `canAutoInstall` always returns true.

The two remaining callers are dead branches:

```64:68:cmd/entire/cli/versioncheck/autoupdate.go
	if !canAutoInstall() {
		printNotification(w, currentVersion, latestVersion)
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "To update, download the latest release from:\n  %s\n", downloadsURL)
		return autoUpdateActionSkip
	}
```

```486:490:cmd/entire/cli/versioncheck/versioncheck.go
func UpdateCommandForCurrentBinary(currentVersion string) string {
	if !canAutoInstall() {
		return downloadsURL
	}
	return updateCommand(currentVersion)
}
```

The name was already a bit of a lie: even when it returned true, Windows never auto-ran (`goos == windows` still prints and skips). It meant “we have a command to show,” not “we will exec it.” That distinction is now empty.

`downloadsURL` still earns its keep for release notes. `canAutoInstall` does not. I’d delete the function and those two `if !canAutoInstall()` checks on #2217.

